{"step_index":207,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:38:28Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:38:28+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:38:28+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/lean_disproof.py`\nTotal Lines: 291\nTotal Bytes: 13556\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/lean_disproof.py — formal Lean 4 *disproofs* for refuted conjectures.\n3: \n4: The refutation engine already produces the hard part of a disproof: a conjecture\n5: together with a graph on which it fails. Turning that pair into a machine-checked\n6: theorem costs nothing extra — the proof is \"evaluate both sides on this graph\" —\n7: provided the invariants can actually be evaluated by the kernel. They can, via\n8: the computable mirror in ``LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable`` (the\n9: specification-style definitions in ``GraphInvariants.lean`` use ``sInf`` over a\n10: ``Set`` and are noncomputable, so ``decide`` stalls on them).\n11: \n12: The emitted shape is a negated universal discharged at the witness::\n13: \n14:     theorem CEGIS_disproof_ab12 :\n15:         ¬ (∀ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n16:              (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj],\n17:              GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G →\n18:              GraphCalc.totalZeroForcingNumber G ≤ GraphCalc.zeroForcingNumber G) := by\n19:       intro h\n20:       have := @h (Fin 5) _ _ Gcex _ (by decide)\n21:       revert this\n22:       decide\n23: \n24: Scope mirrors ``lean_export``: a conjecture whose invariants or classes are not\n25: in the computable layer is skipped rather than mis-formalized, and so is a\n26: witness too large to evaluate (the subset enumerations are exponential in the\n27: vertex count).\n28: \"\"\"\n29: from __future__ import annotations\n30: \n31: import hashlib\n32: import re\n33: from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n34: \n35: from pipeline\n<truncated 2122 bytes>\nlc.IsSubcubicClass\",\n70:     \"eulerian\":       \"GraphCalc.IsEulerianClass\",\n71:     \"triangle_free\":  \"GraphCalc.IsTriangleFreeClass\",\n72:     \"K_4_free\":       \"GraphCalc.IsK4FreeClass\",\n73:     \"connected\":      \"GraphCalc.IsConnectedClass\",\n74:     \"tree\":           \"GraphCalc.IsTreeClass\",\n75:     \"claw_free\":      \"GraphCalc.IsClawFreeClass\",\n76:     \"cograph\":        \"GraphCalc.IsCographClass\",\n77:     # Computable mirror of the spec-layer `G.Colorable 2`, which has no\n78:     # Decidable instance; validated against graphcalc before being listed.\n79:     \"bipartite\":      \"GraphCalc.IsBipartiteClass\",\n80: }\n81: \n82: # Every invariant is a minimum/maximum over subsets of V, so evaluation is\n83: # exponential in the vertex count, and past some size the kernel check stops\n84: # being worth attempting. The refutation itself is still recorded either way.\n85: #\n86: # The previous value of 8 turned out to measure the *heartbeat limit* rather\n87: # than feasibility: order-9 witnesses all failed at ~26 s with `maxHeartbeats`\n88: # exceeded, but one verified in 387 s once heartbeats were unbounded (see the\n89: # preamble). 10 is deliberately conservative — beyond it the cost has not been\n90: # measured, and an unbounded heartbeat count means a bad case hangs until the\n91: # subprocess timeout rather than failing fast.\n92: MAX_WITNESS_ORDER = 10\n93: \n94: _REL_LEAN = {\"≤\": \"≤\", \"≥\": \"≥\", \"<=\": \"≤\", \">=\": \"≥\", \"=\": \"=\"}\n95: \n96: # `maxRecDepth` raises the *elaborator's* recursion limit only; it does not\n97: # weaken the kernel check that follows. The whole proof is evaluation, and the\n98: # class tests enumerate vertex tuples: the cograph test is four nested\n99: # quanti