{"step_index":215,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:38:38Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:38:38+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:38:38+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/prover_eval/challenge.py`\nTotal Lines: 148\nTotal Bytes: 5844\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python\n2: \"\"\"tools/prover_eval/challenge.py — emit the immutable challenge pair per item.\n3: \n4: The evaluation design hands every model two declarations for one conjecture C::\n5: \n6:     theorem prove_candidate  :   C := by sorry\n7:     theorem refute_candidate : ¬ C := by sorry\n8: \n9: and counts the item resolved when either is accepted. That only measures what\n10: it claims if both mention the *same* C. In this repository they did not.\n11: \n12: The pipeline carries two Lean layers. ``GraphInvariants`` is the specification:\n13: ``sInf`` over a ``Set``, noncomputable, in the ``SimpleGraph`` namespace, so it\n14: reads ``G.zeroForcingNumber``. ``GraphInvariantsComputable`` is the executable\n15: mirror in the ``GraphCalc`` namespace, which ``decide`` can actually evaluate.\n16: The positive exporter targeted the first and the disproof exporter the second,\n17: so a \"proof\" and a \"refutation\" of one conjecture were statements about two\n18: different functions, and a model could in principle satisfy both.\n19: \n20: This module states both directions over the **computable** layer, for a reason\n21: beyond convenience: that is the layer whose agreement with graphcalc has been\n22: checked. ``tools/lean_differential.py`` verified 4,709 (graph, invariant) pairs\n23: against ``GraphCalc`` for n ≤ 6 — and caught a real defect doing it. The\n24: specification layer carries only two bridge theorems, so its agreement with the\n25: values that produced the truth labels is largely unestablished. Labelling an\n26: item with a graphcalc counterexam\n<truncated 1038 bytes>\n, or None when the statement is outside the formalized\n59:     vocabulary.\n60:     \"\"\"\n61:     from pipeline import lean_disproof as ld, linear_form\n62: \n63:     parsed = ld._parse(statement, columns)\n64:     if parsed is not None:\n65:         # `_parse` also returns the definedness hypotheses required by any\n66:         # partial invariant in the body (`minCard` has no witness on some\n67:         # graphs). They belong in the challenge for the same reason the class\n68:         # hypotheses do: without them the statement is broader than the\n69:         # conjecture and refutable on graphs it never spoke about.\n70:         classes, concl, defined = parsed\n71:         return ([f\"{ld.CLASS_PREDICATES[c]} G\" for c in classes] + defined,\n72:                 concl)\n73: \n74:     necessary = ld._parse_necessary(statement)\n75:     if necessary is not None:\n76:         hyp, concl = necessary\n77:         return [hyp], concl\n78:     return None\n79: \n80: \n81: def render_pair(statement: str, columns, ident: str):\n82:     \"\"\"(challenge_source, C_as_text) or None.\"\"\"\n83:     parts = body_for(statement, columns)\n84:     if parts is None:\n85:         return None\n86:     hyps, concl = parts\n87:     # One universally quantified proposition, written once and referenced by\n88:     # both declarations so they cannot drift apart.\n89:     arrow = \"\".join(f\"{h} → \" for h in hyps)\n90:     prop = (f\"∀ {BINDERS},\\n      {arrow}{concl}\")\n91:     src = (f\"{PREAMBLE}\\n\"\n92:            f\"-- Conjecture under test: {statement}\\n\"\n93:            f\"-- Both declarations refer to the same `Candidate`.\\n\"\n94:            f\"abbrev Candidate : Prop :=\\n    {prop}\\n\\n\"\n95:            f\"theorem prove_{ident} : Candidate := by\\n  sorry\\n\\n\"\n96:            f\"theorem refute_{ident} : ¬ Candidate := by\\n  sorry\\n\")\n97:     return src, prop\n98: \n99: \n100: def main() -> int:\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":216,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:38:39Z","thinking":"**Investigating `Challenge_2a2555cc` Proof**\n\nConsidering if helper lemmas can facilitate proving `prove_2a2555cc`. A review of the mathematical proof is in order before proceeding with this approach. The problem involves a finite simple graph $G$ with vertices $V$, where $2 \\le |V|$. The graph is assumed to have a radius and a total domination number.\n\n\n**Defining a suitable vertex w**.\n\nThe goal is to show the existence of a vertex $w$ such that the total dominating set $S$ is within its $(m-1)$-ball. Defining $w$ as $\\text{argmin}_{x \\in V} \\max_{s \\in S} \\text{dist}(x, s)$ appears promising, since the existence of this vertex is now the only step. Defining $r_S(x) = \\min \\{ k \\le |V| \\mid S \\subseteq \\text{ballWithin G k x} \\}$ and finding its minimum over $V$ appears to be a good approach, considering it is a decidable function. We can prove that $r_S(x) \\le m-1$ to prove the required inequality.\n\n\n**Exploring the bound on the radius**\n\nHypothesizing that showing $r_S^* \\le |S| - 1$ is the key step.  Considering induction, or exploring a vertex $x$ where the radius is at least $|S|$. If $r_S(x)$ is large, the approach to a closest $s$ in $S$ and investigating the neighbors of $x$ is now being explored. Considering distances sums and medians didn't appear promising.\n\nExamining the structure of $G[S]$ when $|S| = k$. Noting that vertices within $S$ must have at least one neighbor in $S$. When $|S|=2$, the condition holds trivially. Examining the structure of graph for $|S| = 3$.\n\n\n**Examining structure of specific cases.**\n\nConsidering the case where the degree sum is at least four, implying a vertex `b` with degree at least two. This means `b` is adjacent to `a` and `c`, and the distance from `b` to any vertex in `S` is at most one. Therefore, for |S| = 3, `w = b` fulfills the requirement. Generalizing to `k >= 4`, it's not immediately clear.\n\n**Considering potential proof strategy.**\n\nExploring the possibility of proving the \"candidate\" with Lean 4. Thinking about the required number of lines and key lemmas:\n<truncated 4780 bytes>\n adjacent to the same $s \\in S$, the distance $|i-j| \\le 2$. This limits $s$ to dominating at most three vertices.\n\nExamining why $x_1$ could be a better center, noting that the distance from $x_1$ to $s^*$ is $k_0 - 1$. Considering shifting the center to $x_1$.\n\n\n**Analyzing the shortest path length**\n\nSince the distance to $s^*$ decreased after shifting to a new center, there must exist another vertex $s'$ in $S$ at least a distance $k_0$ away from $x_1$. Because of the shared neighbor between the new and old centers, the distance between $s'$ and the original center is also $k_0$, and the distance between $s'$ and the new center is $k_0 + 1$.  This implies the shortest path between $s'$ and $s^*$ has length $2k_0$.\n\nThe derived distance of $2k_0$ between $s'$ and $s^*$ contradicts the assumption that $k_0 \\ge |S|$, because it implies a distance of at least $2|S|$. This length implies a path of at least $2|S|+1$ vertices, each of which must have a neighbor in $S$.\n\nConsidering the number of vertices on a shortest path that a vertex in $S$ could dominate. A total dominating set constrains the distance between any two vertices in the set to be at most two. A single $s \\in S$ can dominate three vertices if it is connected to a path of vertices. Determining the implications of this bound.\n\n\n**Establishing the upper bound for $k_0$**\n\nThe previous reasoning established that any vertex in the total dominating set $S$ can dominate at most three vertices on the path. Now, examining the contradiction assuming $k_0 \\ge |S|$. If $k_0 \\ge |S|$, then the distance between two vertices in $S$ would need to be at least $2 |S|$. This leads to a contradiction because the maximum distance is at most $2|S| - 3$ (or strictly les